PL011 UART — QEMU virt console
==============================

The QEMU virt machine wires a PrimeCell PL011 UART at `0x09000000`
as its primary console. QEMU's firmware leaves the device already
initialised, so this module only needs the bare minimum: poll the
TX-FIFO-full flag in `UARTFR` and stuff bytes into `UARTDR`.

    module oskit.arch.aarch64.board.virt

PL011 register addresses.

    val UART0_DR:   i64 = 0x09000000
    val UART0_FR:   i64 = 0x09000018
    val UART0_IMSC: i64 = 0x09000038  // interrupt mask set/clear
    val UART0_ICR:  i64 = 0x09000044  // interrupt clear
    val UARTFR_TXFF = 0x20            // bit 5: TX FIFO full
    val UARTFR_RXFE = 0x10            // bit 4: RX FIFO empty
    val UART_RXIM   = 0x10            // IMSC bit 4: receive interrupt mask

Write one byte to the data register, waiting for the TX FIFO to
drain first. `volatile` on the flag pointer keeps the read from
being hoisted out of the busy-wait loop by the optimiser.

    uart_putc(c: int)
        volatile var fr: *i32 = *i32(UART0_FR)
        var busy = 1
        while busy != 0
            busy = int(*fr) & UARTFR_TXFF
        var dr: *i32 = *i32(UART0_DR)
        *dr = i32(c)

Write a NUL-terminated sysl string, translating `'\n'` into CRLF
for terminal emulators that expect carriage returns.

    uart_puts(s: string)
        val p = *byte(s)
        val n = len(s)
        for var i = 0; i < n; i++
            val ch = int(p[i])
            if ch == 10
                uart_putc(13)
            uart_putc(ch)

Poll the RX FIFO. Returns 1 if empty (no data available), 0 if
a byte can be read. Used by the IRQ handler to drain all pending
characters when a receive interrupt fires.

    uart_rx_empty() -> int
        volatile var fr: *i32 = *i32(UART0_FR)
        (int(*fr) & UARTFR_RXFE) >> 4

Read one byte from the RX FIFO. Caller must check `uart_rx_empty()`
first; reading an empty FIFO returns stale data.

    uart_rx_read() -> int
        var dr: *i32 = *i32(UART0_DR)
        int(*dr) & 0xFF

Enable the receive interrupt — the GIC needs to have IRQ 33 (SPI 1)
enabled separately. Call once during init after the GIC is live.

    uart_enable_rx_irq()
        var imsc: *i32 = *i32(UART0_IMSC)
        *imsc = i32(UART_RXIM)

Acknowledge the RX interrupt by clearing the RXIC bit. Call at the
end of the IRQ handler; the GIC EOI still has to be written too.

    uart_ack_rx_irq()
        var icr: *i32 = *i32(UART0_ICR)
        *icr = i32(UART_RXIM)
